  "not_simulated": [
    {
      "what": "data output",
      "why": "DOUT follows DIN 500 ns later, rise and fall alike, once this LED holds its 24 bits, and is low before that and until the next reset. Section 11 gives that delay as a maximum and no typical, and gives no output widths, so DOUT passes each high as long as it came. The sheet gives no DOUT drive strength either; the output stage is 100 ohm to each rail. Bits a Pulses record carried, such as an ESP32-S3 RMT train, reach DIN only when the record ends. Past this LED's 24 bits they go on to the next LED as a record of their own, 500 ns later, and DOUT itself stays low."
    },
    {
      "what": "light",
      "why": "The reading `color` is the 8-bit red, green and blue the LED latched, not its luminous intensity. The sheet rates each colour's intensity separately, and the model does not weigh them. The drawing lights the lens in the latched colour, at full glow when its brightest channel is 255."
    },
    {
      "what": "supply voltage",
      "why": "Section 8 lists VDD from +3.7 V to +5.5 V and states no under-voltage floor. Below 3.0 V the pixel ignores DIN, clears to off (leaving only the faded idle draw, 0 A at 0 V) and releases DOUT, and recovers from the next full frame once VDD returns. The ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 ties this LED to 3.3 V, above 3.0 V and below 3.7 V; the kind does not warn there, and its draw fades below 3.7 V, to 97% at 3.3 V. Section 9 also names SK6805MINI-HS at 5 mA, and the draw is the 12 mA SK6812MINI-HS."
    },
    {
      "what": "bit times",
      "why": "A 0 is a high of 220 ns to 400 ns and a 1 is a high of 580 ns to 1 us. Section 12 of this sheet gives T0H as 0.2 / 0.32 / 0.4 us and T1H as 0.62 / 0.64 / 1.0 us, and section 11 gives fDIN typical 800 kHz. The typical highs and the T0H maximum, 0.4 us, sit inside those windows. A high past 400 ns is dropped."
    }
  ]
}
